# Reach Creature::Dig's division with SkillLevel(SK_MINING) exactly 0 (bd inc-4ht).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#   INCURSION_LOAD=tools/fixtures/chars/gnome-warrior-wis3-pickaxe-seed1-opt0822.sav \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/dig-zero-skill.keys 1
#
# THE CHARACTER is a frozen fixture, bd inc-inr80: a gnome warrior with Wisdom 3
# whose Mining rating is exactly 0, holding the pickaxe in the weapon hand.
# tools/keys/gnome-warrior-pickaxe-save.keys builds him and explains every term
# of that rating. This script builds nothing; it starts in play mode.
#
# WHY THE y VERB MENU AND NOT THE MINING SKILL. Both reach the same function.
# The verb menu is used here because it is deterministic: Player::YuseMenu asks
# Q_DIR, EffectPrompt answers a direction by setting e.EDir and NOT e.EXVal or
# e.EYVal, and Creature::Dig reads e.EXVal/e.EYVal -- which e.Clear() left at
# (0,0). So this dig always lands on map square (0,0), which is Indestructable
# Rock, whose MaterialHardness for AD_PIERCE is -1. -1 is not greater than 0, so
# the "That area is too hard" gate passes on any seed. (That mis-aimed target is
# a second defect and is NOT what this script is about.)
#
# The in-play route reaches the same division: wield the pickaxe, use the Mining
# skill, walk into a wall of solid igneous rock -- material MAT_MAGMA, hardness
# -1 for piercing -- which src/MakeLev.cpp:2180 writes around every magma,
# brimstone or igneous floor. That route needs the right region to generate, so
# it is not what a check should depend on.
#
# WHAT TO READ OFF THE LAST SCREEN. `percent = 100 / SkillLevel(SK_MINING)` and
# Skills.cpp prints max(0, 100 - m->PercentSI) when the dig finishes:
#
#   before the guard   "(100% structural integrity remaining.)"  AArch64 SDIV
#                      answers a zero divisor with 0, so nothing was spent
#   after  the guard   "(0% structural integrity remaining.)"    100/max(1,0)
#
# On x86 there is no "before": IDIV raises #DE and the process dies.

# Photograph the inventory so the check can assert the pickaxe is in the weapon
# hand; Creature::Dig tests InSlot(SL_WEAPON), so carrying it is not enough.
i
@dump:wielded
ESC

y
@choose "Dig"
i
@choose "pickaxe"
UP
@dump:digresult

@quit
